<speak>
<p>Work in the domain A generated over the integers by i, the chosen roots for the distinct primes dividing u, and the reciprocal of u. Here u is odd and greater than one, and A sits inside the complex numbers. The exact divided differences x sub S already belong to A by Node seven point two: divide coefficients exactly, evaluate, then multiply by the unit inverse of c sub S.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>For either family, the product of one plus t x sub S over even subsets equals the product over odd subsets. Expand both sides exactly. Each has a constant one, a linear term t times its own sum of x values, and a remainder divisible by t squared. The two remainders are kept, not discarded as an approximation.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Subtract the odd-subset expansion from the even-subset expansion. The two constant terms cancel. Gather the linear terms into t L, and the difference of the remainders into t squared Q. We obtain t L plus t squared Q equals zero. L is the alternating sum of the x values, with plus signs for even subsets and minus signs for odd subsets. Both L and Q belong to A. Each family has its own L and Q.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For the r family, t equals two. For the w family, t equals one plus i. Both are nonzero in the original domain. Factor the equality as t times the quantity L plus t Q equals zero. Cancel the nonzero scalar there, obtaining the exact equality L equals minus t Q. This uses the domain property; it does not require t to be a unit. No residue has been divided yet.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Only now reduce modulo the ideal generated by one plus i in A. Since two equals the product of one plus i and one minus i, both choices of t have residue zero. The right-hand side minus t Q vanishes, so L is congruent to zero. Both equations reduce in the same quotient ring. That quotient need not be a field or a domain.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Write c sub S for c evaluated at zeta sub S, and similarly for d and g. For the first family, the alternating sum of d sub S minus c sub S, divided by two c sub S, is congruent to zero. For the second, replace that numerator by g sub S minus c sub S, and the divisor by the product of one plus i with c sub S. This sum is also congruent to zero. These fractions denote existing elements of A formed before reduction, not division by zero residues.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>If we reduced the original product equality first, every factor one plus t x sub S would become one. We would see only one equals one and lose the linear information. The useful order is exact expansion, subtraction, cancellation in A, and then reduction.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>For u equal to three, the two subsets give just one factor on each side. Both remainders vanish, so Q equals zero and the alternating sums are zero exactly. The general argument keeps the remainders and obtains the two congruences we need. The next step adds them term by term in the same quotient ring.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
